# Data source: Table of General Standard Chinese Characters (通用规范汉字表)

Upstream: `jaywcjlove/table-of-general-standard-chinese-characters` (npm package
`togscc` 1.3.1, MIT), which transcribes 《通用规范汉字表》 published by the
Ministry of Education of the People's Republic of China on 2013-06-01
(<http://www.moe.gov.cn/jyb_sjzl/ziliao/A19/201306/t20130601_186002.html>).
The table's three levels hold 3500 / 3000 / 1605 characters (8105 total).

Retrieved through two independent channels and compared element-by-element:

1. npm tarball `https://registry.npmjs.org/togscc/-/togscc-1.3.1.tgz`
   → `package/data/characters.json` (8105 entries, in the table's stroke order)
2. jsDelivr GitHub proxy
   `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/jaywcjlove/table-of-general-standard-chinese-characters@main/data/characters.json`

Both lists are equal (`a == b` over all 8105 elements), so the transcription is
not a single-vendor artifact.

Files:

- `level1_3500.txt` — the 3500 level-1 characters, one per line, UTF-8, LF
  endings, no BOM, table order preserved (this is the file the generator reads)

Identity (frozen in `docs/superpowers/designs/202610201517-moon-textstat-移植-数据与门禁约定.md` §1.2
and re-checked by `tools/verify_l1.py`):

- bytes: 14000
- lines: 3500
- sha256: 79a6c710013cc86617d5db65871f59b2d67dee72415d380a2cc7145a51450fe4
- carriage returns: 0

Level-1 membership note: the level-1 set contains 罐 and 瓤 but not 龘, 爨 or 齉
(those sit in level 2 / level 3). This makes the list usable as a discriminating
fixture: a text mixing common and rare characters yields a coverage strictly
between 0 and 1.
